<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Duchy of Swabia</h1><p class="st-meta">915 CE – 1268 CE · capital Ulm</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The stem duchy of south-west Germany, home of the Hohenstaufen emperors, which collapsed with the execution of the last Staufer in 1268.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the death of the Alemannic dukes in 746, Alamannia was governed by counts. In 915 Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erchanger-duke-of-swabia">Erchanger</a> defeated King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conrad-i-of-germany">Conrad I</a>'s forces at Wahlwies and was proclaimed duke; Conrad executed him in 917, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burchard-ii-duke-of-swabia">Burchard II</a> established the duchy, recognised by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-fowler">Henry the Fowler</a> in 919. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swabia">Swabia</a> covered south-west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace">Alsace</a> and eastern <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/swiss-confederation">Switzerland</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Dukes were often royal relatives, and from 1079 the office went to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenstaufen">Staufer</a> family, who became emperors with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conrad-iii-of-germany">Conrad III</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa">Frederick Barbarossa</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swabia">Swabia</a> was rich in monasteries, imperial cities such as Ulm and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-augsburg">Augsburg</a>, and lesser nobles. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knighthood">knightly</a> culture produced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minnesang">Minnesang</a> poets.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After the fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenstaufen">Staufer</a>, the last duke, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conradin">Conradin</a>, was beheaded at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> in 1268. The duchy dissolved into many territories, including the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-wurttemberg">County of Württemberg</a>, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/margraviate-of-baden">Margraviate of Baden</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-habsburg">Habsburg</a> lands and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/free-imperial-city">free imperial cities</a>, which later formed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swabian-league">Swabian League</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swabian-circle">Swabian Circle</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/duchy-of-swabia.jpg" alt="Duchy of Swabia" width="320" height="280"><figcaption>Image: Alphathon /ˈæɫfə.θɒn · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Duchy_of_Swabia_locator_map_(1004).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>915 CE – 1268 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Ulm</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Burchard II, Frederick I of Swabia, Conradin</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Alemannic and Swabian German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>, <a href="/countries/switzerland">Switzerland</a>, <a href="/countries/france">France</a>, <a href="/countries/austria">Austria</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Alemannia, Stem Duchy of Swabia</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1092&amp;focus=duchy-of-swabia" aria-label="Duchy of Swabia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4912 485 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Duchy of Swabia lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5232 741L5212 741M5232 741L5199 691M5232 741L5229 694"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5212 741h0M5199 691h0M5229 694h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5232 741h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5232 741h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Ulm. <a href="/atlas?year=1092&amp;focus=duchy-of-swabia">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
